          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0
    ,
      "why": "unrolled_k with k=2..4 can hit >=1 MSPS at modest Fmax without the register overhead of full pipelining. It trades a little area for throughput headroom, giving the Pareto front a second axis to explore. Same accuracy ranges as iterative since accuracy depends only on data_width/n_iter/guards/rounding."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >=1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts_plus_ffs) as the primary objective. iterative is the area-minimal family and the most likely to dominate the selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs). unrolled_k with small k (2-4) provides throughput headroom at low area cost, populating the Pareto front where iterative might be Fmax-limited. Both families share identical accuracy behaviour, so I focus the search on the data_width/n_iter/guard ranges that satisfy the 10-bit error constraint while keeping area minimal. angle_guard is kept low (-2..0) and frac_guard low (0..1) to minimise area; rounding is explored to capture any accuracy/area trade-off. Budget is split evenly since both families are viable and the front spans both."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established with 4 unique designs. The minimum area design (iterative, W=16, N=15, trunc) achieves 266 LUTs+FFs with 10.6 bits accuracy, comfortably meeting the throughput (11 MSPS >= 1) and accuracy (2^-10.60 <= 2^-10) constraints. The front covers the trade-off space from 266 to 319 area units and 10.6 to 11.5 accuracy bits. Since this is the final round, we stop and select the design with minimum luts_plus_ffs (266) as per the spec's selection rule."
,
